As your Senior Technical Education Mentor, I will provide you with a comprehensive code example to create a simple calculator using HTML for structure, CSS for styling, and **JavaScript** for dynamic functionality.

It's important to clarify a common point of confusion: while you mentioned "Java," for client-side web development (what runs in a user's web browser), the correct language for adding interactivity and logic is **JavaScript**. Java is a distinct, general-purpose programming language primarily used for backend applications, Android development, and large enterprise systems, and it doesn't run directly in the browser in the same way JavaScript does.

Here's how you can build a basic calculator using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript:

### 1. HTML (index.html) - Structure the Calculator

This file defines the layout and elements of your calculator.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Simple Calculator</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div class="calculator">
        <input type="text" class="calculator-display" id="display" disabled>
        <div class="calculator-buttons">
            <button class="button operator" data-operator="clear">AC</button>
            <button class="button operator" data-operator="backspace">DEL</button>
            <button class="button operator" data-operator="%">%</button>
            <button class="button operator" data-operator="/">/</button>

            <button class="button number">7</button>
            <button class="button number">8</button>
            <button class="button number">9</button>
            <button class="button operator" data-operator="*">*</button>

            <button class="button number">4</button>
            <button class="button number">5</button>
            <button class="button number">6</button>
            <button class="button operator" data-operator="-">-</button>

            <button class="button number">1</button>
            <button class="button number">2</button>
            <button class="button number">3</button>
            <button class="button operator" data-operator="+">+</button>

            <button class="button number zero">0</button>
            <button class="button number decimal">.</button>
            <button class="button operator equals" data-operator="=">=</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### 2. CSS (style.css) - Style the Calculator

This file provides the visual design for your calculator, making it look functional and aesthetically pleasing.

```css
body {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 100vh;
    margin: 0;
    background-color: #f0f2f5;
    font-family: 'Arial', sans-serif;
}

.calculator {
    background-color: #222;
    border-radius: 10px;
    box-shadow: 0 10px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
    padding: 20px;
    width: 320px;
}

.calculator-display {
    width: calc(100% - 10px);
    height: 60px;
    background-color: #444;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 5px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 2.5em;
    text-align: right;
    padding: 0 10px;
    color: #fff;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    pointer-events: none; /* Prevent input selection */
}

.calculator-buttons {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 10px;
}

.button {
    background-color: #555;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 5px;
    color: #fff;
    font-size: 1.5em;
    padding: 20px 0;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background-color 0.2s ease;
}

.button:hover {
    background-color: #666;
}

.button.operator {
    background-color: #f79232;
}

.button.operator:hover {
    background-color: #ff9d47;
}

.button.equals {
    grid-column: span 2; /* Make equals button span two columns */
    background-color: #28a745;
}

.button.equals:hover {
    background-color: #2fb94c;
}

.button.zero {
    grid-column: span 2; /* Make zero button span two columns */
}
```

### 3. JavaScript (script.js) - Add Functionality

This file contains the logic that makes the calculator interactive, handling button clicks and performing calculations.

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const display = document.getElementById('display');
    const buttons = document.querySelectorAll('.button');

    let currentInput = '0';
    let previousInput = '';
    let operator = null;
    let resetDisplay = false;

    function updateDisplay() {
        display.value = currentInput;
    }

    function clear() {
        currentInput = '0';
        previousInput = '';
        operator = null;
        resetDisplay = false;
        updateDisplay();
    }

    function backspace() {
        if (currentInput.length > 1) {
            currentInput = currentInput.slice(0, -1);
        } else {
            currentInput = '0';
        }
        updateDisplay();
    }

    function appendNumber(number) {
        if (resetDisplay) {
            currentInput = number;
            resetDisplay = false;
        } else if (currentInput === '0' && number !== '.') {
            currentInput = number;
        } else {
            currentInput += number;
        }
        updateDisplay();
    }

    function chooseOperator(op) {
        if (operator && !resetDisplay) {
            calculate();
        }
        previousInput = currentInput;
        operator = op;
        resetDisplay = true;
    }

    function calculate() {
        if (!previousInput || !operator) return;

        let result;
        const prev = parseFloat(previousInput);
        const current = parseFloat(currentInput);

        if (isNaN(prev) || isNaN(current)) return;

        switch (operator) {
            case '+':
                result = prev + current;
                break;
            case '-':
                result = prev - current;
                break;
            case '*':
                result = prev * current;
                break;
            case '/':
                if (current === 0) {
                    alert("Cannot divide by zero!");
                    clear();
                    return;
                }
                result = prev / current;
                break;
            case '%':
                result = prev % current;
                break;
            default:
                return;
        }
        currentInput = result.toString();
        operator = null;
        previousInput = '';
        resetDisplay = true; // Prepare for next operation
        updateDisplay();
    }

    buttons.forEach(button => {
        button.addEventListener('click', () => {
            const value = button.textContent;
            const dataOperator = button.dataset.operator;

            if (button.classList.contains('number')) {
                appendNumber(value);
            } else if (button.classList.contains('operator')) {
                if (dataOperator === 'clear') {
                    clear();
                } else if (dataOperator === 'backspace') {
                    backspace();
                } else if (dataOperator === '=') {
                    calculate();
                } else {
                    chooseOperator(dataOperator);
                }
            }
        });
    });

    // Initialize display
    updateDisplay();
});
```

### How to Run This Code:

1.  **Create three files:** `index.html`, `style.css`, and `script.js` in the same directory.
2.  **Copy and paste** the respective code into each file.
3.  **Open `index.html`** in your web browser.

### Code Explanation:

*   **`index.html`**:
    *   It links `style.css` in the `<head>` section to apply styling.
    *   It includes a `<div class="calculator">` to contain all elements.
    *   The `<input type="text" id="display" disabled>` serves as the screen where numbers and results appear. `disabled` prevents users from typing directly into it.
    *   The `<div class="calculator-buttons">` holds all the calculator buttons.
    *   Each button has classes (`button`, `number`, `operator`, `equals`, `zero`, `decimal`) for CSS styling and `data-operator` attributes for JavaScript to identify their function.
    *   `<script src="script.js"></script>` is placed just before the closing `</body>` tag. This ensures that the HTML elements are fully loaded before the JavaScript attempts to interact with them.

*   **`style.css`**:
    *   Uses Flexbox (`display: flex`) to center the calculator on the page.
    *   Styles the `calculator` container, `calculator-display`, and individual `button` elements.
    *   Uses CSS Grid (`display: grid`) for the `calculator-buttons` to arrange them neatly in a 4-column layout.
    *   Specific styles are applied to `operator` and `equals` buttons for visual distinction.
    *   `grid-column: span 2;` is used to make the `0` and `=` buttons wider.

*   **`script.js`**:
    *   **`DOMContentLoaded`**: Ensures the script runs only after the entire HTML document has been loaded and parsed.
    *   **Variables**:
        *   `currentInput`: Stores the number currently displayed.
        *   `previousInput`: Stores the first operand of an operation.
        *   `operator`: Stores the chosen arithmetic operator (+, -, \*, /).
        *   `resetDisplay`: A flag to determine if the display should be cleared for a new number.
    *   **`updateDisplay()`**: Updates the `<input type="text">` with the `currentInput` value.
    *   **`clear()`**: Resets all variables to their initial state.
    *   **`backspace()`**: Removes the last digit from `currentInput`.
    *   **`appendNumber(number)`**: Adds a digit or a decimal point to `currentInput`, handling cases like starting with '0' or a new calculation.
    *   **`chooseOperator(op)`**: Sets the `operator` and moves `currentInput` to `previousInput` in preparation for the second operand. If an operator is already active, it performs the previous calculation first.
    *   **`calculate()`**: Parses `previousInput` and `currentInput` as floats, performs the arithmetic operation based on the `operator`, and updates `currentInput` with the result. Includes basic error handling for division by zero.
    *   **Event Listeners**: Loops through all buttons and attaches a `click` event listener to each. Based on the button's class or `data-operator` attribute, it calls the appropriate function (`appendNumber`, `clear`, `backspace`, `chooseOperator`, `calculate`).

### Official Documentation and Verified Developer Guides:

For further in-depth learning and to master these technologies, here are real, authentic, and direct HTTPS links to official documentation:

#### HTML Documentation
*   **MDN Web Docs - HTML:** The primary resource for learning HTML, maintained by Mozilla. [HTML: HyperText Markup Language](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML)
*   **W3C HTML Standard:** The official standard for HTML. [W3C HTML Standard](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/)

#### CSS Documentation
*   **MDN Web Docs - CSS:** Comprehensive guide to CSS properties, selectors, and concepts. [CSS: Cascading Style Sheets](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS)
*   **CSS-Tricks:** A popular resource for CSS tips, tutorials, and examples. [CSS-Tricks](https://css-tricks.com/)

#### JavaScript Documentation
*   **MDN Web Docs - JavaScript:** The definitive guide to JavaScript for web development. [JavaScript (JS)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript)
*   **ECMAScript Standard (ECMA-262):** The official standard for the JavaScript language. [ECMAScript® 2024 Language Specification](https://tc39.es/ecma262/) (You can navigate to previous versions from here).
*   **Google Developers - Web Fundamentals (JavaScript):** Practical guides and best practices for using JavaScript in web development. [Google Developers - JavaScript](https://developers.google.com/web/fundamentals/primers/javascript)

By following this guide, you now have a functional calculator and the resources to deepen your understanding of modern web development using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Keep practicing, and don't hesitate to explore more complex features!

